#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""wave-usage <wave-transcript-dir> — emit backfill rows JSON for a completed wave.

THE TRANSCRIPT-DERIVED TOKEN PATH, and a designed one rather than a workaround:
a claimant cannot observe its own token consumption, so the relay that spawned
it measures the transcripts here and pipes the result to `docket dispatch
backfill-usage --from-json -`. The engine's at-source channel, `docket step
record --usage`, covers the different case — units a claimant CAN measure.

Joins each agent-<id>.jsonl's usage to its step id (named in the agent's first
user message — the bootstrap prompt) and sums the four typed units per step.
Usage entries are deduplicated BY MESSAGE ID before summing: streamed assistant
messages repeat across transcript lines, and a per-line sum double-counts
(measured 1.65-2.36x on a past wave before the dedup).

DEDUP TAKES THE LAST OCCURRENCE OF A MESSAGE ID, NOT THE FIRST, and that
direction is load-bearing — do not "simplify" it back to a seen-set. Streaming
rewrites one message id across many lines and `output_tokens` GROWS with each
rewrite, so the first occurrence is a PARTIAL count. Taking the first
under-reported output ~3x (measured on one step: 4,488 recorded against
14,691 actual) while input and both cache units, which never change across
occurrences, matched exactly — which is why it went unnoticed. The original
double-count this dedup was written to fix was driven by those cache units
(cache_read per-line 3,823,178 vs 2,231,212 true, 1.71x, right in the band
above); output_tokens per-line was already within ~1% of correct. So the two
failure modes pull in opposite directions and last-wins is what satisfies both.

`--exclude STEP-N` (repeatable) drops a step whose usage a PRIOR dispatch's
backfill already carries — a gate probed in one wave and seated in the next
emits usage in both journals, and the engine's append-only refusal is
per-batch, not per-row.

`--seats` keys the rows by (PROPOSAL, SEAT) instead of by step, for a panel the
conductor convened itself, and emits `{"proposal", "voter", "unit", "quantity"}`
for `docket vote backfill-usage --from-json -`. A tribunal seat carries a
proposal id and never a step id, so `dispatch backfill-usage` has nothing to
attach to and this spend had no ledger path at all: in-wave panels ARE counted,
because their seats run as steps, while conductor-side panels of identical shape
recorded nothing. The difference was only WHERE the ballot executed, not how much
work it did.

THE MODES PARTITION THE AGENTS ON TWO TESTS OF THE BOOTSTRAP BRIEF — was this
agent told to cast a ballot, and was it told to record a step? — so running both
over a wave counts every agent at most once and loses none silently:

  * an agent BRIEFED TO CAST is emitted only by `--seats`, keyed by its
    (proposal, seat); plain mode auto-skips it (naming it on stderr) because
    emitting it under its step too would double-count the judges;
  * an agent BRIEFED TO RECORD A STEP is emitted only by plain mode, keyed by
    the step its own `docket step claim/record STEP-N` obligation names;
    `--seats` names it on stderr and drops it, because the engine refuses usage
    for a voter with no cast and misfiling it onto a seat that did cast would be
    worse than losing it;
  * an agent briefed to do NEITHER is wave overhead. Neither mode emits it;
    plain mode sums it and prints one WAVE OVERHEAD line on stderr.

DO NOT JOIN ON THE FIRST STEP-N IN THE BRIEF. Every executor bootstrap
names its assignment in a `docket step record STEP-N` obligation, and THAT is the
join — the same string the claimant is required to pass, so the join cannot
disagree with the record, exactly as the seats join reads the cast. A bare
STEP-N search cannot tell "execute this step" from "read this step": wave.js
probes a step's row before claiming it (`docket step show STEP-N --json`), and
under the old first-match join each such probe back-filled ~17K mostly-cache
tokens onto the step it PROBED — measured on RUN-66's wf_68b7e3e1-abe, where
STEP-3146 (pending, never claimed), STEP-3158 (superseded) and STEP-3166 got
whole ledger rows for steps no agent ever ran, and STEP-3156/STEP-3157 carried
their probe's spend on top of their executor's. The step id a probe names is
kept only to LABEL it in the overhead report.

The overhead half is not just the silent-seat checker: EVERY wave spawns agents
that execute nothing — the pre-claim probe, the gate's show/tally/record/target
reads, the fix-round ancestry reads, the block-probe that recovers a null
spawn's reason, and wave.js's own staged-closure polling. They are haiku-tier
and individually small, but they are on every wave. Before the per-agent split
both modes threw them away — seats mode because they never cast, plain mode
because the conductor `--exclude`d the vote step wholesale to keep the judges
from being counted twice. That exclusion is no longer needed and MUST NOT be
reinstated: the skips are per-agent, so a conductor runs plain mode over vote
steps and `dispatch backfill-usage` carries every agent that really recorded
one. `--exclude` keeps its own, unrelated purpose below — dropping a key a PRIOR
dispatch's backfill already carries.

The PROPOSAL half of that key is what makes one run of the script serve a
multi-panel wave: one wave now seats several panels (the staged closure rides
vote rows) with the SAME seat names on each, so a voter-only key cannot be split
per proposal — and `vote backfill-usage` takes one proposal at a time. Rows
carry the proposal so the caller can `jq` the one output per panel instead of
hand-deriving the mapping. `--exclude` still names a bare SEAT in this
mode and drops it under every proposal, which is its documented contract.

Overhead is reported, never guessed at: each such agent is named on stderr with
what it read and what it cost, under one WAVE OVERHEAD total. That report is the
whole point of the third bucket — a wave's probe spend is real, it simply has no
step that owns it, and printing it beats both misfiling it and dropping it
silently.

Deterministic, read-only, stdout-only. Exit 1 if an agent's bootstrap cannot be
read at all, if an agent briefed as a step executor names no step to record (the
brief and the join have drifted), or if a dispatched agent carries no usage —
silence must not look like success.
"""
import json, os, re, sys

# The markers that identify an agent spawned to READ rather than to execute.
# Containment (not a prefix test) because list-shaped message content arrives
# JSON-encoded, and every constant must stay newline-free AND PURE ASCII — the
# brief's line breaks arrive as literal backslash-n in that encoding, and its em
# dashes as literal \\u2014.
#
# PROBE_MARKER is the explicit declaration wave.js's probeBrief() now opens with,
# and is the marker to key on going forward (seam (b)). The other three
# are the shapes already deployed in transcripts that predate it, so a back-fill
# over an OLD wave classifies correctly too: probeBrief's fixed opening line plus
# its self-description (truncated deliberately — wave.js writes "what the record
# currently says" and tribunal.js's checker "what the VOTE record currently
# says"; both are probes), and blockProbeBrief's opening sentence.
PROBE_MARKER = "WAVE PROBE: not a step execution"
PROBE_PREFIX = "Run exactly this one command:"
PROBE_MARK = "read-only probe reporting what the"
BLOCK_PROBE_MARK = "You are a DIAGNOSTIC PROBE inside a running Docket wave"

# The opening line of wave.js's bootstrap() — the ONE brief that makes an agent
# a claimant. It is the safety net under RECORD_RE: an agent that opens with
# this and yet carries no claim/record command means the brief was reworded out
# from under the join, and every executor in the wave would otherwise fall
# silently into the overhead bucket, emptying the ledger while exiting 0.
EXEC_MARK = "You are executing one step of a Docket run"


def is_probe(bootstrap: str) -> bool:
    """Was this agent briefed to READ the record rather than advance it?"""
    return (PROBE_MARKER in bootstrap
            or BLOCK_PROBE_MARK in bootstrap
            or (PROBE_PREFIX in bootstrap and PROBE_MARK in bootstrap))

# The three things a bootstrap brief can say about what its agent is for.
#
# CAST_RE and RECORD_RE are the JOINS, and both are read on every run in either
# mode, because the two answers together are what split the wave into judges,
# claimants, and overhead — one pass, one partition, so an agent cannot be
# emitted twice or fall between the modes.
#
# Each join reads the very command the agent is REQUIRED to run, so it cannot
# disagree with what the engine recorded: the seat from `docket vote cast`, the
# step from `docket step claim`/`record`. The proposal id and the seat name come
# from ONE match on the cast command, never from two searches: a brief that
# names a second cast anywhere would otherwise pair one line's proposal with
# another line's voter.
#
# STEP_RE is NOT a join. It is a loose "which step is this agent
# looking at" read, used only to label an overhead agent in the report — a probe
# names the step it READS, which is precisely the id that must not be joined on.
STEP_RE = re.compile(r"STEP-\d+")
CAST_RE = re.compile(r"docket vote cast\s+(\S+)\s+--voter\s+(\S+)")
RECORD_RE = re.compile(r"docket step (?:claim|record|complete)\s+(STEP-\d+)")

UNITS = {
    "input_tokens": "input_tokens",
    "output_tokens": "output_tokens",
    "cache_creation_input_tokens": "cache_creation_tokens",
    "cache_read_input_tokens": "cache_read_tokens",
}

USAGE = ("usage: wave-usage [--seats] [--exclude STEP-N|SEAT]... "
         "<transcript-dir>")

HELP = f"""{USAGE}

Emit backfill rows JSON for a completed wave, joined from the agent transcripts
in <transcript-dir> and deduplicated by message id. Read-only; stdout only.

  --seats           key rows by (proposal, voter), read from each judge's own
                    `docket vote cast <proposal> --voter <seat>` command, for
                    `docket vote backfill-usage --from-json -`. Emits ONLY the
                    agents briefed to cast. Without it rows are keyed by the step
                    each agent's own `docket step claim/record STEP-N`
                    obligation names, for `docket dispatch backfill-usage
                    --from-json -`, and cover ONLY the agents that were briefed
                    to record a step. An agent briefed to do neither — every
                    read-only probe wave.js spawns — is WAVE OVERHEAD: it is
                    summed and reported on stderr, never keyed to the step it
                    happened to read. The modes partition the wave:
                    run both, count each agent once, and do not `--exclude` vote
                    steps to compensate.
  --exclude VALUE   drop a key a prior back-fill already carries: a STEP-N, or
                    in --seats mode a bare SEAT name, dropped under every
                    proposal. Repeatable.
  -h, --help        print this and exit 0.

Exits 1 if an agent's bootstrap cannot be read, an agent briefed as a step
executor names no step to record, or a dispatched agent carries no usage; 2 on a
usage error.
"""

def main() -> int:
    argv = sys.argv[1:]
    if "-h" in argv or "--help" in argv:
        print(HELP, end="")
        return 0
    exclude: set[str] = set()
    seats = False
    if "--seats" in argv:
        seats = True
        argv.remove("--seats")
    while "--exclude" in argv:
        i = argv.index("--exclude")
        if i + 1 >= len(argv):
            print(USAGE, file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        exclude.add(argv[i + 1])
        del argv[i:i + 2]
    if len(argv) != 1 or not os.path.isdir(argv[0]):
        print(USAGE, file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    d = argv[0]
    # --seats keys rows by SEAT instead of by step, for a panel the conductor
    # convened itself. A tribunal seat carries a proposal id and never a step
    # id, so `dispatch backfill-usage` has nothing to attach to and this spend
    # had no ledger path at all: in-wave panels are counted because their seats
    # run as steps, while conductor-side panels of identical shape recorded
    # nothing (measured ~40k output tokens per panel that no run budget saw).
    # `docket vote backfill-usage --from-json -` takes these rows.
    #
    # The proposal id and the seat name are read from the cast command the brief
    # hands the judge — `docket vote cast <id> --voter <seat>` — which is the
    # same string the seat is required to pass, so the join cannot disagree with
    # the cast. Plain mode reads that SAME cast to decide what to leave alone,
    # so the agent seats mode emits is exactly the agent plain mode skips.
    totals: dict[tuple[str, str] | str, dict[str, int]] = {}
    errors: list[str] = []
    unattributed: list[str] = []
    seated: list[str] = []
    overhead: list[tuple[str, str]] = []
    overhead_sums = {u: 0 for u in UNITS.values()}
    for name in sorted(os.listdir(d)):
        if not (name.startswith("agent-") and name.endswith(".jsonl")):
            continue
        path = os.path.join(d, name)
        step = None
        cast = None
        bootstrap = None
        per_msg: dict[str, dict] = {}
        with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as f:
            for line in f:
                try:
                    obj = json.loads(line)
                except json.JSONDecodeError:
                    continue
                if ((step is None or cast is None)
                        and obj.get("type") == "user"):
                    content = obj.get("message", {}).get("content")
                    text = content if isinstance(content, str) else json.dumps(content)
                    if bootstrap is None:
                        bootstrap = text or ""
                    if step is None:
                        m = STEP_RE.search(text or "")
                        if m:
                            step = m.group(0)
                    if cast is None:
                        m = CAST_RE.search(text or "")
                        if m:
                            cast = m.groups()
                if obj.get("type") == "assistant":
                    msg = obj.get("message", {})
                    usage = msg.get("usage")
                    mid = msg.get("id")
                    if not usage or not mid:
                        continue
                    per_msg[mid] = usage  # last write wins — see module docstring
        agent_sums = {u: 0 for u in UNITS.values()}
        for usage in per_msg.values():
            for src, dst in UNITS.items():
                agent_sums[dst] += int(usage.get(src) or 0)
        if seats:
            if cast is None:
                # A vote wave spawns agents that never cast — the relays, the
                # tally reader, the conditional-row probe, tribunal.js's
                # silent-seat checker. Their spend is real but attaches to no
                # seat, and the engine refuses usage for a voter that never
                # cast, so it is reported and dropped here rather than misfiled
                # onto a seat that did cast. PLAIN MODE CARRIES IT, keyed by
                # step — the two modes partition the wave.
                unattributed.append(name)
                continue
            key: tuple[str, str] | str = cast
        else:
            if cast is not None:
                # This agent cast a ballot, so `--seats` emits it keyed by
                # (proposal, seat). Emitting it here under its step as well
                # would count the judges twice — skip it, and say so, so a
                # conductor never has to `--exclude` a vote step to compensate.
                seated.append(f"{name} ({cast[0]}/{cast[1]})")
                continue
            if bootstrap is None:
                errors.append(f"{name}: no user message — cannot classify")
                continue
            # THE STEP JOIN. An agent executes a step only if its
            # brief obliges it to claim/record that step, and the obligation
            # names the id — so read the id from the obligation rather than
            # grabbing the first STEP-N in the prose. Read it from the BOOTSTRAP
            # ONLY, never from later user messages: those carry tool results,
            # and a probe's own `docket step show` output can quote a step
            # artifact that names the record command. What briefs the agent is
            # what decides this; what the agent read back cannot.
            m = RECORD_RE.search(bootstrap)
            if m is None:
                # Briefed neither to cast nor to record: this agent advanced no
                # step, so its spend is wave overhead — reported below, and NOT
                # keyed to whatever step its brief happens to mention. Keying on
                # that mention is what put ~17K tokens apiece on a pending and a
                # superseded step; a probe names the step it READS.
                #
                # The one shape that must never land here is an executor. If a
                # brief reword ever breaks RECORD_RE, every claimant would fall
                # into this bucket and the wave would back-fill NOTHING while
                # exiting 0 — so an agent carrying the executor opening with no
                # claim/record command is a hard error, not overhead.
                if EXEC_MARK in bootstrap:
                    errors.append(f"{name}: opens as a step executor but names "
                                  f"no `docket step claim/record STEP-N` — the "
                                  f"bootstrap brief and RECORD_RE have drifted")
                    continue
                what = ("read-only probe" if is_probe(bootstrap)
                        else "not a wave agent")
                overhead.append(
                    (name, f"{what}{f', mentions {step}' if step else ''}"))
                for u, q in agent_sums.items():
                    overhead_sums[u] += q
                continue
            key = m.group(1)
        # In seats mode the key is (proposal, seat) but `--exclude` names a bare
        # SEAT, unchanged from before the proposal half existed: one exclusion
        # drops that seat under every proposal in the directory.
        if (key[1] if seats else key) in exclude:
            continue  # a prior back-fill already carries this step/seat
        if not any(agent_sums.values()):
            label = "/".join(key) if seats else key
            errors.append(f"{name} ({label}): transcript carries no usage")
            continue
        t = totals.setdefault(key, {u: 0 for u in UNITS.values()})
        for u, q in agent_sums.items():
            t[u] += q
    for name in unattributed:
        print(f"wave-usage: {name}: no seat (never cast) — dropped here; plain "
              "mode carries it if it recorded a step, and reports it as wave "
              "overhead if it did not", file=sys.stderr)
    for label in seated:
        print(f"wave-usage: {label}: cast a ballot — skipped here, carried by "
              "seats mode (run with --seats)", file=sys.stderr)
    for name, what in overhead:
        print(f"wave-usage: {name}: {what} — recorded no step, so its usage is "
              "wave overhead and is attributed to none", file=sys.stderr)
    if overhead:
        print(f"wave-usage: WAVE OVERHEAD: {len(overhead)} agent(s), "
              + ", ".join(f"{u} {q:,}" for u, q in overhead_sums.items())
              + " — no step owns this spend; it is reported, not back-filled",
              file=sys.stderr)
    if errors:
        for e in errors:
            print(f"wave-usage: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    # Tuples sort lexicographically, so seats-mode rows come out grouped by
    # proposal — which is the order the per-proposal `jq` downstream wants.
    order = (lambda k: k) if seats else (lambda k: int(k.split("-")[1]))
    rows = [
        {**({"proposal": key[0], "voter": key[1]} if seats else {"step": key}),
         "unit": unit, "quantity": qty}
        for key in sorted(totals, key=order)
        for unit, qty in totals[key].items()
    ]
    json.dump(rows, sys.stdout, indent=2)
    print()
    return 0

if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
